WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
Cloud Code. That's the focus of this lesson. Specifically, what kind of tool it actually is,

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.399
how it differs from IDE plugins, how the planning loop runs, how the context window fills up,

3
00:00:12.399 --> 00:00:17.680
and what happens when it does, and what the permission model controls. Understanding

4
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.559
the architecture is what makes the rest of this course make sense altogether.

5
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.840
Cloud Code lives in your terminal, and that placement is the whole design.

6
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:35.119
It's Anthropx Agentix CLI. It executes multi-step tasks autonomously using a loop of planning,

7
00:00:35.119 --> 00:00:42.000
tool selection, execution, and observation. The planning loop is that core cycle. Cloud

8
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.720
reasons about what to do next, picks a tool, runs it, reads the result, then decides what to do next.

9
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.080
It keeps looping until the task is complete, and the permission model is the layered control system

10
00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.319
that determines which tools and actions Cloud Code is actually allowed to perform. It draws

11
00:01:00.319 --> 00:01:08.800
on user, project, local, and managed settings, with managed settings taking precedence, and deny rules,

12
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:15.040
overriding allow rules. Permissions are enforced before any tool executes, which is the foundation

13
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:21.279
everything else builds on. The most important framing to lock in early. Cloud Code isn't a

14
00:01:21.279 --> 00:01:27.919
chat interface, and it's not an autocomplete tool. It's an autonomous agent. When you give it a task,

15
00:01:27.919 --> 00:01:34.559
it doesn't respond with a suggestion for you to apply. It executes. It reads files, runs shell

16
00:01:34.559 --> 00:01:40.639
commands, edits code, and searches the web. A single instruction can trigger dozens of tool calls

17
00:01:40.639 --> 00:01:47.360
before it's done. The practical implication is that Cloud Code can do a lot of damage if pointed

18
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.559
at the wrong thing with the wrong permissions. It can also complete a lot of real work with no

19
00:01:52.559 --> 00:01:57.919
hand-holding. The autonomous execution model is what makes it powerful, and what makes the

20
00:01:57.919 --> 00:02:04.239
permission model worth understanding carefully. IDE plugins like Copilot work by watching your

21
00:02:04.239 --> 00:02:09.919
keystrokes and offering inline suggestions that you can accept or reject. Cloud Code doesn't do

22
00:02:09.919 --> 00:02:15.360
that. It receives a goal, reasons about what steps are needed to achieve it, and takes action

23
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:22.479
autonomously across the entire code base. Plugins have no shell access. Cloud Code can run arbitrary

24
00:02:22.479 --> 00:02:28.960
bash commands, read any file it has permission to read, and make changes across multiple files in a

25
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:35.759
single task. The mental model is plugins assist one action at a time. Cloud Code plans and executes

26
00:02:35.759 --> 00:02:40.960
multi-step work independently. That distinction matters because it changes how you think about

27
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:47.600
what to ask for and what guardrails to put in place. The planning loop runs in four phases.

28
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:53.600
First, Cloud plans. It reasons about the current state of the task and decides what the next action

29
00:02:53.600 --> 00:03:00.000
should be. Second, it makes a tool call invoking the specific tool with the parameters it determined.

30
00:03:00.639 --> 00:03:06.479
Third, it observes the result from that tool call and updates its understanding of the current state.

31
00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:13.440
Fourth, it loops back to planning and decides the next step. This cycle repeats until the task

32
00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:19.119
is complete or Cloud hits a decision point where it needs clarification. The loop is what makes

33
00:03:19.119 --> 00:03:25.679
Cloud Code agentic. It's not waiting for instructions between steps. It's continuously planning and

34
00:03:25.679 --> 00:03:32.320
acting until the goal is reached. It helps to contrast the agentic loop with a standard single

35
00:03:32.399 --> 00:03:39.600
shot LLM call. A single shot call is you send input, you get a response, you're done. No follow-up

36
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:45.759
actions, no ability to observe results and adjust. That model works fine for generating text or

37
00:03:45.759 --> 00:03:51.679
answering a question. It breaks down the moment a task requires intermediate steps where each step's

38
00:03:51.679 --> 00:03:58.720
output shapes what comes next. The agentic loop handles that. Cloud plans the first step, executes

39
00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:04.800
it, reads what happened, and adjusts. If a tool call produces an unexpected result, the loop lets

40
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.720
Cloud self-correct before proceeding. That's the core difference in capability between a chat

41
00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:17.200
interface and an agentic execution environment. The context window is the finite amount of text

42
00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:22.880
Cloud Code can hold in a session. Everything competes for that space. The conversation history,

43
00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:31.119
the results from every tool call, and any Cloud.md files that were loaded. Verbose Cloud.md files

44
00:04:31.119 --> 00:04:36.959
directly reduce how much space is left for code and tool output. When the window fills up, earlier

45
00:04:36.959 --> 00:04:43.519
content gets compacted or dropped. The practical design implication is to keep Cloud.md files

46
00:04:43.519 --> 00:04:49.119
focused and concise. Every word you write there is a word that can't be used for code context or

47
00:04:49.119 --> 00:04:54.399
tool results. This is a real constraint, not a theoretical one, and it shapes how you should

48
00:04:54.399 --> 00:05:01.200
structure project configuration. Cloud Code doesn't automatically carry your full conversation history

49
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:07.839
into a new session. What does carry forward is anything written down. Cloud.md files load

50
00:05:07.839 --> 00:05:13.440
automatically and bring project context with them because Cloud Code reads them from disk

51
00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:19.040
at the start of every session, not because they live in session memory. Where auto memory is

52
00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:25.359
enabled, saved notes carry forward too, and any explicit summary you write out to a file before

53
00:05:25.359 --> 00:05:31.040
ending the session is there when you come back. Without those, a new session has no automatic

54
00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:37.279
recall of the prior chat. The design implication is simple. Don't rely on Cloud Code to remember

55
00:05:37.279 --> 00:05:43.119
loose conversation from a prior session. Write state explicitly, commit configuration, and

56
00:05:43.119 --> 00:05:50.000
structure your Cloud.md to carry what matters forward. The permission model controls what

57
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:56.399
Cloud Code is allowed to do. Permissions come from settings at several levels, user, project,

58
00:05:56.399 --> 00:06:02.559
local, and managed enterprise settings. When those levels conflict, managed settings win,

59
00:06:02.559 --> 00:06:09.040
and a deny rule always overrides an allow rule. By default, certain actions require explicit

60
00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:16.079
approval before they run. Now, hooks and permissions are two separate gates, and the order matters.

61
00:06:16.079 --> 00:06:22.480
A pre-tool use hook runs first, before the permission system is consulted. So a hook gets a

62
00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:28.000
chance to deny an action before permissions are even checked. Both are pre-execution gates,

63
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:34.239
sitting in front of the tool call rather than after it. That's the mental model to carry. Pre-tool

64
00:06:34.239 --> 00:06:39.920
use hook, then the permission request, then the tool runs. Getting both right is what makes Cloud

65
00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.359
Code safe to run in automated workflows where it won't be supervised step by step.

66
00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:53.200
People hear Cloud Code and picture autocomplete with a better vocabulary. The real difference is

67
00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:59.359
that it acts, reads the result, then decides what to do next, over and over until the work is done.

68
00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:05.760
Once a tool takes real actions on your machine, you start caring about things you'd never ask

69
00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:10.079
of autocomplete. What does it remember, and what is it allowed to touch?

70
00:07:10.799 --> 00:07:13.760
That's a wrap on this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

